publication type = {Journal article; Conference proceeding},
keywords = {*digital twin; *physiological stress; Adult; Algorithm; Anticipation; Anxiety; Conference abstract; Controlled study; Depression; Diagnosis; Digital transformation; Emotion; Female; Health care cost; Health care management; Health survey; Human; Interview; Male; Mental health; Mental health service; Perceived Stress Scale; Prevention; Psychological well‐being; Qualitative analysis; Randomized controlled trial; Reaction time; Self concept; Signal detection; Stress management; Timeliness; Visual stress; Wearable device; Workplace},
abstract = {INTRODUCTION: Smart city and digital transformation have intensified mental health challenges for urban workers, yet traditional interventions face limitations in addressing dynamic needs due to latency and fragmented scenarios. Digital twin technology provides innovative solutions by creating real‐time virtual‐physical mappings of urban and individual environments, enabling proactive stress monitoring and crisis anticipation. This study evaluates the Digital Twin‐based Stress Visualization System (DTSVS) to validate its effectiveness in real‐time mental health surveillance, predictive accuracy, and intervention efficacy, offering technical insights for smart city health management frameworks. METHODS: A randomized controlled trial (RCT) was conducted with 300 occupational participants (aged 25‐45, from IT, finance, healthcare sectors) in a smart city pilot zone, split into intervention (n=150) and control groups (n=150). The intervention group used a Digital Twin Stress Visualization System (DTSVS) integrating wearable biometric data, office digital twin modeling, and AI‐driven emotion recognition algorithms to generate real‐time stress heatmaps with tiered alerts for users and enterprise mental health services. The control group maintained routine mental health protocols. Outcomes were measured using the Perceived Stress Scale (PSS), Depression Anxiety Stress Scale‐21 (DASS‐21), and system satisfaction surveys. Pre/post intervention data over 12 weeks, combined with user interviews, analyzed the system's crisis signal detection rate, response speed, and psychological status changes. RESULTS: The results indicate that the DTSVS system can significantly improve the effectiveness of psychological crisis warning. The intervention group showed a 32.7% decrease in stress levels and a 41.3% improvement rate in depression and anxiety symptoms, which was higher than the control group's 6.7% (P<0.001). The accuracy of the system's warning for high‐risk psychological states reached 89.2%, and the average response time was shortened to 18.7 hours. The qualitative analysis results showed that 76.3% of users believed that visual stress feedback enhanced self‐awareness, and 68.4% of managers reported that the system improved the accuracy of organizational intervention. CONCLUSION: Research has shown that the digital twin stress visualization system significantly improves the accuracy and timeliness of psychological health interventions for the workplace population through dynamic data‐driven, multimodal feedback, and intelligent warning mechanisms. Based on individual environment coupling analysis, it provides scientific basis for organizational stress management. Future research can further explore the long‐term intervention effects and algorithm optimization paths of the system across industries and cultures, and promote the transformation of mental health services towards an active prevention model .},
DOI = {10.1097/01.yco.0001119200.07131.57},
URL = {https://www.cochranelibrary.com/central/doi/10.1002/central/CN-02874522/full}
